commute=add('lem-normal-surface-normalization-commutes-with-base-completion','Normalization of a surface modification commutes with local-base completion',['lem-surface-finite-type-formal-fibres','lem-surface-regular-fibres-preserve-normality','lem-surface-finite-type-normalization-finite','thm-completion-of-a-noetherian-local-ring','thm-integrality-commutes-with-localisation','def-normal-surface-modification-and-normalized-point-blowup'],r'''Let $A$ be a normal local surface domain essentially of finite type over a field or a complete equicharacteristic local base, with normal completion $\widehat A$. For an integral modification $X$ of $\operatorname{Spec}A$, finite normalization commutes with the base change to $\widehat A$: $(X^\nu)_{\widehat A}$ is the finite normalization of $X_{\widehat A}$. Both base-changed schemes are integral, with common function field $\operatorname{Frac}\widehat A$.''',r'''On an affine chart of X put B⊂K=Frac(A), and let Bν be its finite normalization in K. Flatness of Ahat over A injects B⊗A Ahat and Bν⊗A Ahat into K⊗A Ahat, the localization of the domain Ahat at the nonzero elements of A. Thus both are domains. Their fraction fields are Frac(Ahat): B contains A, lies in K and localizing B by nonzero A elements gives K, so the same after flat base change gives that common field. A→Ahat has geometrically regular formal fibres by the exact finite-type formal-fibre helper. The map Bν→Bν⊗A Ahat is its flat base change; its fibres are base-field extensions of those formal fibres (the residue extensions are finitely generated because Bν is finite type over A after clearing the given localization). They are regular by geometric regularity. Normality ascent along flat regular-fibre maps therefore makes Bν⊗A Ahat normal. It is finite and integral over B⊗A Ahat and lies in their common fraction field. Any element of that field integral over the smaller ring is also integral over this normal ring and hence belongs to it. Thus it is exactly the normalization. These identifications agree on localizations inside the common function field and glue. This proof uses the actual geometrically regular completion map and finite normalization; it does not invoke formal gluing or assume normality of an arbitrary completed ring.''')
desc=add('lem-normalized-surface-point-blowup-resolution-descends-from-completion','Normalized point sequences and resolutions descend from completion',['lem-surface-completion-base-change-preserves-closed-fibre-local-completions',commute,'thm-blowup-base-change-flat','lem-proper-surface-regularity-transfers-to-and-from-completion','lem-surface-finite-type-normalization-finite'],r'''For $A$ as in the preceding normalization-completion lemma, every finite sequence of normalized point blowups over $\operatorname{Spec}\widehat A$ has a uniquely corresponding finite sequence over $\operatorname{Spec}A$ with isomorphic base-changed models. Each centre lies over the closed point. If the completed terminal scheme is regular, so is the descended terminal scheme. Singular centres correspond to singular centres.''',r'''The starting models are normal A and Ahat. At each step, properness sends its closed centre to the closed point of the local base. The closed fibres of the corresponding models are canonically identified, so this centre corresponds to a unique closed point of the original model, with the same residue field. The matching centre ideal commutes with completion base change: on an affine chart the closed point prime contains mA, and quotienting by m identifies the fibre prime; flat base change therefore gives its extended prime exactly. Blowups commute with that flat base change by the published blowup theorem. Their finite normalizations commute by the preceding helper, establishing the model identification inductively. Their projectivity over the local affine base is preserved by blowups and finite normalization. If the completed terminal model is regular, the proper regularity-transfer helper descends regularity to the original terminal scheme. At the centres themselves the equal-completed-local-ring helper and regularity of a ring iff its completion is regular prove equivalence of regularity, hence of singularity. No algebraic descent of arbitrary modification data is claimed: only the finite point sequences and their matching fibre ideals are descended.''')
globalize=add('lem-normal-finite-type-surface-resolution-globalizes-from-complete-local-points','Surface resolution globalizes from complete local point resolutions',['lem-surface-open-regular-locus','lem-normal-domain-implies-r-one','lem-surface-regular-fibres-preserve-normality',desc,'lem-local-normalized-point-blowup-sequences-spread-at-closed-points','thm-blowup-projective','lem-finite-over-projective-noetherian-affine-base-is-projective'],r'''Let $Y$ be a normal integral finite-type surface over any field. If each completed local ring at a singular closed point has a regular resolution by finitely many normalized point blowups at singular centres, then $Y$ has such a finite global sequence, proper and birational over $Y$ and an isomorphism on the regular locus.''',r'''The regular locus is open by the exact regular-locus helper; normality makes every codimension-one local ring regular. Its closed complement therefore has dimension zero and consists of finitely many closed points in the Noetherian surface. Each local ring there has dimension two and normal completion by normality ascent. For each such point, the preceding helper descends its given completed point-blowup resolution to a finite local sequence, preserving singularity of the centres. Spread that local sequence globally by the closed-point spreading helper. All its centres are over that original singular point and it is an isomorphism away from it. The local terminal scheme is regular; consequently the global terminal scheme is regular at every point over that original point, because localization over its local spectrum identifies those local rings. Perform these finite sequences successively at the finitely many original singular points. The sequences over later points lie in regions where all earlier operations were isomorphisms, so retain the same local input and do not alter already resolved regions. The final scheme is regular over all original singular points and over the unchanged regular locus, hence everywhere. Each step is proper (projective blowup followed by finite normalization), integral and birational; their finite composite has the asserted properties. No properness of Y over its field is assumed and no smoothness over an imperfect field is inferred.''')
